WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.140
I’ve never been really much of a fan of music videos

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.460
and I think that’s because I sort of just like my music as music.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.320
I like to close my eyes and focus on what’s happening sonically.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.000
Because that’s what music is: it’s something that you hear.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.860
So, that means I can totally be engrossed and in love with Beyonce’s latest album

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.960
and not care at all about the dozen videos that came out with it.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.340
But I don’t know, maybe I’m in the minority here.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.600
And the truth is, that, despite music being an aural medium,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.060
it’s long had an intimate relationship with the visual.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:45.000
So I want to talk about perhaps the most iconic visual supplement in the history of music.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.800
But, before we talk about that, what about album covers in general?

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.700
These days we perceive them as a kind of visual statement like the album title or the song names

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.560
that help us situate the tone or the feeling of what we're going to hear.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:06.000
Granted that it stays within the dimension of a square, the options and styles are basically limitless.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.260
But in the beginning, record packaging was purely utilitarian.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:16.160
The early 78-RPM gramophone records were mostly outfitted with brown paper or cardboard sleeves

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.340
to protect the recording.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:25.140
It wasn't until 1938 when Columbia Records hired Alex Steinweiss as its very first art director

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.700
that the idea of cover art came into existence.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.980
Against the plain or traditional design of early covers, Steinweiss' original, eye-catching art

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:41.320
initiated just a huge boom in sales and the market followed, as it always does when something works.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.360
Record stores were refigured so that instead of just seeing the bindings, like books on a shelf,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.500
consumers were encouraged to flip through the covers.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.020
This all points to just how important the cover art was to the marketing of the product.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.160
And music *is* a product, it's a commodity that people consume.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:06.060
And since you can't buy the band itself, and since the music has no spatial dimension,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:12.180
the album cover emerged as the stand-in for the commodity to be purchased.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.000
This explains the rise of photography on album covers in what's called 'the peronality cover'.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.900
You know, one of those covers where it's just a photograph of the band or musician,

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.440
still pretty popular today.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.260
A perfect example of this personality cover can be seen on the Beatles' first album 'Please Please Me'.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.380
Just look at those bright, smiling faces staring warmly into the camera lens.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.880
As Susan Sontag once  brilliantly said,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.580
"In its simplest form, we have ina a photograph

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.540
surrogate possession of a cherished person or thing"

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:50.720
As their career advanced, The Beatles seemed to become darkly aware of their status as commodities.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.840
Perhaps nowhere more plainly acknowledged than on the cover of their fourth album, aptly titled "Beatles For Sale"

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.500
Featuring another example of the personality cover, this time without the warmth and the smiles.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.580
By the time Sgt. Pepper's emerged,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:11.580
The Beatles had already begun challenging the conventions of popular music and its role in culture

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.060
And this extended to the album covers, too.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.020
First, in Rubber Soul, with its distorted image and funky typeface,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.780
Then with Revolver, for which they employed their friend and artist Klaus Voormann

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.860
who did a kind of 1960s update of Victorian illustrator

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.060
Aubrey Beardsley's famous black ink drawings.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:36.060
In other words, The Beatles were leaders in expanding an album cover's function

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.080
from a marketing tool, to a work of art in its own right.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.380
And that brings us back to what is really the Holy Grail of album covers:

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.120
The Beatles' 'Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band.'

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.640
A cover that explicitly engages the audience in so many ways:

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.660
in critiques, in interpretations, in comparisons, in contrasts;

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.380
It's a cover that invites you in.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:06.640
A perfect example of this is the immediate challenge to just identify who all of these people are.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.800
What do Indian gurus, Karl Marx, and Bob Dylan all have in common?

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.480
The Sgt. Pepper's cover wants you to ask questions like this.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.820
Now in one sense, of course, these people just represent The Beatles' interests and influences.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.240
Many of whom didn't make the final cut.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:29.180
And yet, by juxtaposing highbrow artists and thinkers, like Marx, or Aubrey Beardsley,

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.900
or George Bernard Shaw or Dylan Thomas

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:37.920
with pop icons, Bob Dylan, Marilyn Monroe, Marlon Brando, and Shirley Temple,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.100
The Beatles signal the breakdown and mixing of high and low culture

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.660
that they themselves exemplified more than any other artist.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:51.860
With Sgt. Pepper, The Beatles were in a posture of challenge,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.240
challenging everything they'd been up to that point,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.060
challenging the highbrow art critics to abandon their exclusion of popular culture,

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.480
challenging the listening public to engage

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:05.120
with something other than love songs in the same four chords.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.260
They were even challenging the feelings of unity

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.020
that defined the summer of love in 1967,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:15.480
delivering a suite of songs that featured alienation and loss

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.860
as much as it did love and togetherness.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.400
The album cover echoes these challenges and adds

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:22.600
some of its own

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.180
by using the technique of photomontage

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.140
which manipulates photography's unique ability to depict reality,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.980
The Beatles blend and collapse

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.360
the values of a number of different times and places.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.340
By staging the scene as a performance and an audience,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.960
the band challenges us to dwell on the function of both.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.120
Indeed, these considerations were at the front of their minds as The Beatles had only recently elected

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.760
to stop performing live, after the mayhem of Beatlemania

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.920
began to diminish the quality of the music.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.740
While some accuse them of leaving their fans behind,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.380
the Sgt.Pepper's cover suggests the opposite:

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.420
that the decision to stop touring was out of respect for their fans

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.320
not out of fear or contempt.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.360
We should also note that the designer of this cover, Peter Blake,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.040
was a noted pop artist,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.760
a style that used aspects of mass culture

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:19.700
to bring attention to the fuzzy lines between art, advertising, news, and all other forms of media.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:25.180
The pop artist wanted to get people to think about the process and manufacturing of art,

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.620
and how its categorizations and hierarchies are largely arbitrary.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:35.320
Blake and The Beatles designed the Sgt. Pepper cover as both a piece of art, and a product:

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.420
inside its gatefold sleeve, the record had paper cutouts of the Pepper Band's regalia,

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.860
adding to its consumer kitch value.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.800
And yet, it was also the first album to display the lyrics on the packaging,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.620
encouraging listeners to interrogate the record's intellectual content as well.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.820
Now the truth is, of course, that music,

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.720
perhaps more prominently than any other art form

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.560
plays both parts:

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.520
as a commodity, and an aesthetic object.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.920
And in many ways, the two functions are inseparable.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.420
The Beatles knew this, now maybe they regretted it,

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.820
but they were self-aware enough to see that it was inescapable.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.780
And if music had to be marketed,

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:17.080
and if the visual was going to be the vehicle of that,

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:22.960
John, Paul, George, and Ringo were going to be as playful here as they were with everything else.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:29.960
In the end this photograph of photographs levels a critique on representation itself.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.840
To show how music is mediated by the visual,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.980
the Beatles put themselves on this cover three times.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.160
Once as the wax figures of the mop-top Beatles that they were,

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.500
once as the fake Pepper band, in its bright and eye-catching glory,

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:47.920
and finally, as themselves,

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.040
pictured not on the cover of their album, but by the effects of that cover.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.820
In the minds of everyone who looked at it.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:59.000
The real Beatles can't be represented this way, not really.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:02.400
Not like this, or like this, or like this.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.940
The cover of Sgt Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band points to the truth,

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.080
what the Beatles themselves knew:
['A Day in the Life' starts playing]

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.180
that all music

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:15.940
is invisible.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.360
John Lennon: [I read the news today, oh boy]

